        <mxCell id="nKA0zrrdCLjoVEnoJHJr-180" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=#b46504;fillColor=#fad7ac;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="Any of these" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="86" x="2342" y="3878.5" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="nKA0zrrdCLjoVEnoJHJr-181" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=#ae4132;fillColor=#fad9d5;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="Poor choice" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="34" width="143" x="2294" y="3614" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="nKA0zrrdCLjoVEnoJHJr-182" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=#ae4132;fillColor=#fad9d5;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="Better choice" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="34" width="143" x="2316" y="3839" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="nKA0zrrdCLjoVEnoJHJr-183" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="&lt;div&gt;An **Optional IRE Condition** allows you to add an extra filter (a SQL `WHERE` clause equivalent) to an Identifier Entry, instructing the Identification and Reconciliation Engine (IRE) to only evaluate that identification rule against a specific subset of CMDB records.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Normally, an IRE rule matches incoming payload values directly against fields in target records across the whole table. When an optional condition is applied, the IRE will **only check existing CIs that meet that filter criteria**, ignoring the rest.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;---&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;### The Problem It Solves&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;By default, an IRE Identifier Entry uses an exact match on specified fields (e.g., `serial_number`, `mac_address`, or `install_directory`).&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;However, CMDBs frequently contain records that share identifiers or have legacy/junk data that you do not want incoming discovery data to match against.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Without an optional condition:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* If the incoming payload matches an existing record that is **retired**, **virtual**, or in a **different lifecycle stage**, IRE might update that stale/retired CI instead of inserting a brand-new active CI.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* If you have custom applications or devices where an identifier (like an IP address or instance name) is reused across environments (e.g., Test vs. Production), IRE might accidentally bind a Test payload to a Production CI.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;---&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;### What the System Property Does&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* **Default Behavior:** ServiceNow only lets you add optional filter conditions to **Lookup-type** identifier entries (entries that resolve relationships in another table, like Network Adapters or Serial Numbers).&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* **Setting `glide.identification_engine.enable_identifier_optional_condition = true`:** This unlocks the **&quot;Advanced Options&quot;** section on **non-lookup** identifier entries directly on the CI table itself. You can now define conditions on regular fields (like `install_status`, `operational_status`, `environment`, etc.).&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;---&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;### Concrete Real-World Example&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;#### Scenario: Active Servers vs. Decommissioned Servers&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;You are discovering Linux servers using ServiceNow Discovery or an external inventory tool.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* **The Issue:**&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* A server named `app-srv-01` was decommissioned last month. Its CI record is set to `Operational Status = Retired`.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* The infrastructure team spins up a brand new server with the same hostname: `app-srv-01`.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* Without optional conditions, IRE matches on `name = app-srv-01`, re-opens/updates the old retired CI, overwrites its history, and changes its status back.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;#### The Solution Using Optional Condition:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;1. Enable `glide.identification_engine.enable_identifier_optional_condition = true`.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;2. Navigate to **CI Class Manager** $\rightarrow$ **Linux Server** $\rightarrow$ **Identification Rule**.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;3. Under the Identifier Entry matching on `name`:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* Set **Optional Condition** to:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;`Operational Status != Retired` (or `Install Status != Retired`).&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;#### How IRE Behaves Now:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* **Incoming Payload:** Hostname `app-srv-01`.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* **IRE Evaluation:**&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;1. IRE searches the `cmdb_ci_linux_server` table for `name = app-srv-01` **AND** `Operational Status != Retired`.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;2. Because the existing `app-srv-01` record has `Operational Status = Retired`, it is excluded from the search pool.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;3. No match is found among active CIs.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;4. IRE **creates a new CI** for the new server instead of corrupting the audit trail of the decommissioned one.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;---&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;### Another Example: Multi-Instance Applications&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Connecting back to multiple software instances:&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* You might have two distinct classes or operational zones (e.g., `Environment = Production` vs. `Environment = Staging`).&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;* Adding an optional condition `Environment = Production` ensures that payload data coming from your production orchestrator will never match, overwrite, or collide with a staging instance installed at the same path (`C:\App\`) on a test box.&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="1045" width="1306" x="2818" y="3513" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
